1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,044
Zoya puts up one poster for everyone.

2
00:00:03,044 --> 00:00:04,249
Healthy laddoos!

3
00:00:04,549 --> 00:00:05,652
Parents stop.

4
00:00:05,652 --> 00:00:06,864
Gym-goers shrug.

5
00:00:06,864 --> 00:00:09,137
Office workers walk right past.

6
00:00:09,687 --> 00:00:12,632
One message for everyone
often reaches no one.

7
00:00:13,282 --> 00:00:14,755
Her survey showed why.

8
00:00:14,755 --> 00:00:17,070
Different people want different things.

9
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:18,671
Parents like Mrs.

10
00:00:18,671 --> 00:00:21,591
Rao want a healthy sweet
for the tiffin box.

11
00:00:21,941 --> 00:00:24,605
Gym-goers want protein after a workout.

12
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:28,316
And office workers want a
small snack at four o'clock.

13
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:31,082
So what is market segmentation?

14
00:00:31,382 --> 00:00:36,331
Kotler and Armstrong call it dividing
a market into smaller groups of buyers,

15
00:00:36,331 --> 00:00:39,759
with different needs,
characteristics or behaviours.

16
00:00:40,109 --> 00:00:45,999
The idea was first named by Wendell
Smith, in a 1956 article in the Journal

17
00:00:45,999 --> 00:00:47,251
of Marketing.

18
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:50,354
In simple words: don't
treat everyone the same.

19
00:00:50,354 --> 00:00:52,800
Group the people who want the same thing.

20
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:55,645
Let's watch Zoya segment her market.

21
00:00:55,995 --> 00:00:59,933
First, she lays out all one
hundred and fifty survey answers.

22
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:02,477
Then she sorts them by one question.

23
00:01:02,477 --> 00:01:04,546
When and why do you eat a snack?

24
00:01:04,896 --> 00:01:08,144
Answers about school
tiffins move into one group.

25
00:01:08,494 --> 00:01:11,297
Answers about workouts
form a second group.

26
00:01:11,647 --> 00:01:14,240
And answers about office
breaks form a third.

27
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:17,354
Now she gives each
group a name and a size.

28
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:19,428
Tiffin parents, sixty.

29
00:01:19,428 --> 00:01:20,771
Gym-goers, forty.

30
00:01:20,771 --> 00:01:22,548
Office snackers, fifty.

31
00:01:23,198 --> 00:01:25,215
A market is not one crowd.

32
00:01:25,215 --> 00:01:26,683
It is many groups.

33
00:01:27,383 --> 00:01:30,638
Marketers usually split
a market in four ways.

34
00:01:30,988 --> 00:01:31,919
Geographic.

35
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:33,360
Where people live.

36
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:36,024
Like homes near schools in Bengaluru.

37
00:01:36,374 --> 00:01:37,289
Demographic.

38
00:01:37,289 --> 00:01:38,375
Who they are.

39
00:01:38,375 --> 00:01:41,371
Like parents of children
aged five to twelve.

40
00:01:41,721 --> 00:01:42,776
Psychographic.

41
00:01:42,776 --> 00:01:43,956
How they live.

42
00:01:43,956 --> 00:01:46,847
Like families who care about healthy food.

43
00:01:47,197 --> 00:01:48,097
Behavioural.

44
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,128
What they do.

45
00:01:49,128 --> 00:01:52,078
Like when they eat,
and how often they buy.

46
00:01:52,728 --> 00:01:54,856
But is every group worth serving?

47
00:01:54,856 --> 00:01:56,983
A good segment passes five tests.

48
00:01:57,333 --> 00:01:58,233
Measurable.

49
00:01:58,193 --> 00:01:59,534
You can count it.

50
00:01:59,534 --> 00:02:00,434
Accessible.

51
00:02:00,394 --> 00:02:01,734
You can reach it.

52
00:02:02,084 --> 00:02:02,984
Substantial.

53
00:02:02,932 --> 00:02:05,002
It is big enough to earn money.

54
00:02:05,002 --> 00:02:05,990
Differentiable.

55
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,180
It really wants something different.

56
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:09,561
And actionable.

57
00:02:09,561 --> 00:02:11,466
You can build a plan for it.

58
00:02:11,466 --> 00:02:13,453
All three of Zoya's groups pass.

59
00:02:14,103 --> 00:02:16,071
Where do we see this at work today?

60
00:02:16,421 --> 00:02:17,321
Geographic.

61
00:02:17,283 --> 00:02:20,944
A food delivery app shows
different dishes in each city.

62
00:02:21,294 --> 00:02:22,222
Demographic.

63
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:25,260
A bank offers a special
account for students.

64
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:26,628
Psychographic.

65
00:02:26,628 --> 00:02:31,042
A phone maker sells a tough phone
for people who love the outdoors.

66
00:02:31,392 --> 00:02:32,295
Behavioural.

67
00:02:32,295 --> 00:02:36,133
A bakery gives a loyalty card
to people who buy every day.

68
00:02:36,783 --> 00:02:39,005
But is segmentation always good?

69
00:02:39,355 --> 00:02:40,470
The good side.

70
00:02:40,470 --> 00:02:43,472
Money and messages go
to the people who care.

71
00:02:43,822 --> 00:02:44,976
The hard side.

72
00:02:44,976 --> 00:02:47,181
Too many tiny groups cost more.

73
00:02:47,181 --> 00:02:49,692
And labels can turn into stereotypes.

74
00:02:50,042 --> 00:02:52,622
Dev assumes only mothers pack tiffins.

75
00:02:52,972 --> 00:02:56,762
But the survey shows many fathers
and grandparents pack them too.

76
00:02:57,112 --> 00:02:59,967
So the group is tiffin
parents, not just mothers.

77
00:03:00,317 --> 00:03:01,217
The lesson?

78
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:04,622
Group people by what they
need, not by what you assume.

79
00:03:05,272 --> 00:03:06,237
Quick check.

80
00:03:06,237 --> 00:03:11,005
Zoya groups buyers by when they snack:
tiffin time, after the gym, or at

81
00:03:11,005 --> 00:03:11,971
four o'clock.

82
00:03:11,971 --> 00:03:13,404
Which base is this?

83
00:03:15,304 --> 00:03:16,204
Behavioural!

84
00:03:16,164 --> 00:03:18,357
It groups people by what they do.

85
00:03:18,957 --> 00:03:19,970
So, remember.

86
00:03:20,270 --> 00:03:24,399
Segmentation divides a market
into groups with different needs.

87
00:03:24,749 --> 00:03:29,947
There are four bases: geographic,
demographic, psychographic and behavioural.

88
00:03:30,297 --> 00:03:33,824
And a good segment can be measured,
reached and served with a plan.

89
00:03:34,424 --> 00:03:36,891
Next time, we'll explore market targeting.
